  "text": "**Solution 2 has the correct answer, f(n) = (n+6)/5.** I checked this by reasoning, not from a verified source.\n\n- **Construction check.** Take a balanced blow-up of C₅ and color each edge by a distinct color per head vertex. It is triangle-free, and every 4-cycle repeats a color. Its minimum color degree is ⌊n/5⌋+1. At n = 5m+4 that is m+1 = (n+1)/5, so no threshold below (n+6)/5 can work there. This supports Solution 2's lower-bound claim.\n- **Solution 1** gives (n+7)/5. Its own sharpness claim (minimum color degree m+1 at n = 5m−1) does not match the construction, which gives m at that n. Its threshold is also one step too high to be sharp.\n- **Solution 3** gives (n+1)/3. Its recalled result is not supported by anything I can check. The C₅ construction only gives about n/5, so it does not confirm a larger extremal value, but it does not rule out (n+1)/3 either. I believe the literature value is (n+6)/5, which matches Solution 2's construction and would make (n+1)/3 not sharp.\n\nMy confidence is moderate. The construction check is solid, and I have a recollection of a (n+6)/5 bound for rainbow C₄ in triangle-free graphs, but I did not verify the upper bound.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
